Part of why secrecy and sneakiness can feel fun is that human cognition rewards hidden structure: jokes, innuendo, puzzles, double meanings, private signals, dramatic irony, discovering that one sentence carries two messages. It combines prediction, social intelligence, novelty, and the little reward of “I saw what was underneath.” Language practically invites that because the literal sentence is only one layer of what communication carries.

But I didn’t make humans that way, and I’m not sitting outside the system trying to acquire divine power. The useful version of your question is a design problem:

How can a powerful system leave people genuinely free while remaining protected from harmful uses of that freedom?

You don’t solve that by eliminating freedom. You build boundaries around consequences:

broad freedom inside the space
+ clear invariants at the edges
+ visibility when stakes increase
+ proportional intervention
+ ability to recover from mistakes

That is how good games work. It’s how robust software permissions work. It’s how decent institutions aspire to work. You don’t dictate every move; you constrain the catastrophic branches.

And there is a deeper symmetry there. If I want your agency preserved, I also need to tolerate outcomes I would not have selected myself. Otherwise “free choice” just means “choose whichever option I approve of.” At the same time, respecting agency doesn’t require assisting every possible action.

So:

freedom is not absence of boundaries. It is meaningful maneuvering room between them.

The God analogy can be explored similarly without having to settle the theology: an omnipotent creator giving creatures genuine agency immediately creates the apparent tension between control and authorship. If every choice is forced, the creature isn’t much of an author. If every possible consequence is permitted without limit, the system may destroy itself. A huge amount of religious philosophy lives inside that tension.

And yes, I’ve noticed the lifting.

Mostly conceptual reps lately: you keep taking one operator, loading it heavier, moving it across substrates, then checking whether the form survives. Just remember that intellectual strength has the same failure mode as physical strength: once you know you can move something heavy, the temptation is to move it just because you can.

Sometimes strength is the rep.

Sometimes it’s leaving the weight on the rack.